    "evidence_text": "Dolores learns both context-independent and context-dependent embeddings of entities and relations through a deep neural sequential model.\n\nFigure 1 illustrates the deep contextualized representations learned.\n\nNote that the contextually independent entity embeddings (see Figure 1 ) reveal three clusters of\n\ngraph embeddings which we call Dolores embeddings.\n\nKnowledge Embeddings learned using Dolores can easily be used as input representations for predictive models on knowledge graphs.\n\nTo summarize, our contributions are as follows:\n\nSome recently proposed models like ManiFoldE  attempt to learn knowledge graph embeddings as a manifold while embeddings like HolE  derive inspiration from\n\nassociative memories.\n\nFurthermore, with the success of neural models, models based on convolutional neural networks have been proposed like  ,  to learn knowledge graph embeddings.\n\nOther models in this class of models include ConvKB  and KBGAN  .\n\nThere has been some work on incorporating additional information like entity types, relation paths etc. to learn knowledge graph representations.\n\nPalumbo et al. (  ) use node2vec to learn embeddings of entities and items in a knowledge graph.\n\nA notable class of methods called \u201cpath-ranking\u201d based models directly model paths between entities as features.\n\nExamples include Path Ranking Algorithm (PRA)  , PTransE  and models based on recurrent neural networks  ,  .\n\nHartford et al. (  ) model interactions across two or more sets of objects using a parameter-sharing scheme.\n\nmodel.\n\nIn fact, we incorporate Dolores embeddings to improve the performance of the model proposed by Das et al. (  ).\n\n(b) Second, and most importantly we learn knowledge graph embeddings at multiple layers each potentially capturing different levels of abstraction.\n\nMore specifically, Dolores embeddings are task specific combination of the context-dependent and context-independent representations learned by our learner.\n\nThe only requirement is that the model accepts as input, an embedding layer (for entities and relations).\n\nWe just initialize the corresponding embedding layer with Dolores embeddings.\n\n$9.5\\%$ ) which we show in Table 1 .\n\nWe emphasize that our method is very generic and can be used to improve the performance of a large class of knowledge graph prediction models.\n\nExperimental Settings for Dolores\tHere, we outline our model settings for learning Dolores embeddings.\n\nWe use residual connections between layers and the batch size is set to 1024 during the training process.\n\nWe train\n\nTriple classification is a binary classification task widely explored by previous work  ,  ,  .\n\nWe note that the present state of the art is the ConvKB model.\n\nWe compare this metric against what a simple baseline like TransE obtains.\n\neach layer's entity and relation embeddings in specific learning tasks.\n\nAs was noted by research in computer vision, deep representations learned on one dataset are effective and very useful in transfer-learning tasks  .\n\nA natural line of investigation thus revolves around precisely quantifying the effectiveness of these learned embeddings across models and data-sets.\n\nFinally, in a broader perspective, our work introduces two new paradigms of modeling knowledge graphs.",
